# Testing Supabase code with supabase_test

## Rules

- Test against a real client from `testSupabaseClient`, or
  `initializeTestSupabase` in a Flutter widget test. Never mock or subclass
  `SupabaseClient`, `AuthClient` or the query builders, and never subclass
  `http.BaseClient` for Supabase requests.
- Answer requests with the `stub…` methods of `MockSupabaseHttpClient`.
  Stub the rows the code under test should receive: filters are not evaluated.
- Put a client into a signed-in state with `signInTestUser`. Build auth
  payloads with `testUserJson` and `testSessionResponseJson`, and tokens with
  `unsignedTestJwt`. Never hand-assemble a JWT or session JSON.
- Assert on what was sent with `requests` and `requestsTo`, not by wrapping
  the client.
- Dispose every client at the end of the test.
- Match methods with `HttpMethod.post` and friends.

## Setup

Add `supabase_test` as a dev dependency, next to `test`, or `flutter_test` in
a Flutter app:

```sh
dart pub add dev:supabase_test dev:test
```

Use `flutter pub add dev:supabase_test` in a Flutter app, and import
`package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart` instead of `package:test/test.dart`.
The examples query the `Books` table of the schema `supabase_typegen`
generates into `lib/supabase_schema.g.dart`. Replace `my_app` with the name
of the package under test.

```dart test-file
import 'package:my_app/supabase_schema.g.dart';
import 'package:supabase/supabase.dart';
import 'package:supabase_test/supabase_test.dart';
import 'package:test/test.dart';

void main() {
  late MockSupabaseHttpClient httpClient;
  late SupabaseClient supabase;

  setUp(() {
    httpClient = MockSupabaseHttpClient();
    supabase = testSupabaseClient(httpClient: httpClient);
    addTearDown(supabase.dispose);
  });

  test('loads the books in print', () async {
    httpClient.stubTable(
      Books.table.name,
      rows: [
        {'id': 1, 'author_id': 2, 'title': 'Dune', 'in_print': true},
      ],
    );

    final books = await supabase
        .table(Books.table)
        .selectOnly([Books.id, Books.title])
        .where(Books.inPrint.isTrue());

    expect(books.single.read(Books.title), 'Dune');
  });
}
```

The stubbed rows are the JSON PostgREST would return, with database column
names, so a typed query decodes them exactly like a real response. Stub every
column the query reads: a `select()` that reads all columns of `BooksRow`
needs every non-nullable column in each row.

## Stubbing

| Endpoint                             | Stub                                                                                                       |
| ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| A table                              | `stubTable('books', rows: [...])`                                                                          |
| A database function                  | `stubRpc('book_by_id', body: {...})`                                                                       |
| An edge function                     | `stubEdgeFunction('hello', body: {...})`                                                                   |
| Sign in, sign up, sign out, the user | `stubSignIn()`, `stubSignUp()`, `stubSignOut()`, `stubUser()`                                              |
| Storage                              | `stubStorageUpload`, `stubStorageDownload`, `stubStorageSignedUrl`, `stubStorageList`, `stubStorageRemove` |
| Any other path                       | `stub(body, method: HttpMethod.get, path: '/storage/v1/bucket/avatars')`                                   |
| A response computed from the request | `stubHandler((request) => jsonResponse(...), path: ...)`                                                   |
| No network                           | `stubError(ClientException('Offline'))`                                                                    |
| A request that never answers         | `stubStall(path: ...)`                                                                                     |
| Retries                              | `stubStatuses([503, 503, 200], body: [...], path: ...)`                                                    |

- The latest matching stub wins, so register defaults in `setUp` and override
  them in a test.
- `times:` limits how often a stub answers. Register the later responses
  first to answer a sequence.
- `query:` narrows a stub to requests carrying those query parameters, for
  example `query: {'id': 'eq.1'}`.
- An unmatched request throws a `StateError` naming the request and the
  registered stubs, and fails the test even when a client wraps the error.
- `testSupabaseClient` does not retry, so a failure surfaces at once. Pass
  `retryOptions` to test retry handling.
- `single()` receives the only row of a one-row stub, and a count query
  receives the number of rows, or `count:` when it is given.

Errors are stubbed with the status code and the service's error body:

```dart test
final httpClient = MockSupabaseHttpClient()
  ..stubTable(
    'books',
    rows: {'message': 'permission denied', 'code': '42501'},
    statusCode: 403,
  );
final supabase = testSupabaseClient(httpClient: httpClient);

await expectLater(
  () => supabase.table(Books.table).select(),
  throwsA(isA<PostgrestApiException>()),
);
```

## Asserting on requests

```dart test
final httpClient = MockSupabaseHttpClient()..stubTable('books');
final supabase = testSupabaseClient(httpClient: httpClient);

await supabase
    .table(Books.table)
    .insert(BooksInsert(authorId: 2, title: 'Dune'));

final insert = httpClient
    .requestsTo('/rest/v1/books', method: HttpMethod.post)
    .single;
expect(insert.jsonBody, {'author_id': 2, 'title': 'Dune'});
```

`requests` holds every answered request with its body already read,
`queryParameters` and `headers` are on each one, and `reset()` clears stubs
and recorded requests.

## Auth

Run a test as a signed-in user without any network traffic:

```dart test
final httpClient = MockSupabaseHttpClient()..stubTable('books', rows: []);
final supabase = testSupabaseClient(httpClient: httpClient);

final session = await signInTestUser(
  supabase.auth,
  userId: 'user-1',
  email: 'someone@example.com',
);
await supabase.table(Books.table).select();

expect(
  httpClient.requests.last.headers['Authorization'],
  'Bearer ${session.accessToken}',
);
```

To test a sign-in flow itself, stub the endpoint and call the real method:

```dart test
final httpClient = MockSupabaseHttpClient()
  ..stubSignIn(user: testUserJson(id: 'user-1'));
final supabase = testSupabaseClient(httpClient: httpClient);

final session = await supabase.auth.signInWithPassword(
  email: 'someone@example.com',
  password: 'password',
);
expect(session.user.id, 'user-1');
```

## Realtime

Pass a `MockRealtimeTransport`, an in-memory realtime server, and push server
events from the test:

```dart test
final realtime = MockRealtimeTransport();
final supabase = testSupabaseClient(
  httpClient: MockSupabaseHttpClient(),
  realtimeTransport: realtime.call,
);

final channel = supabase.channel('books');
final changes = channel.onTableChanges(Books.table);
channel.subscribe();
await channel.onStatusChange.firstWhere(
  (change) => change.status == RealtimeSubscribeStatus.subscribed,
);

realtime.emitPostgresChange(
  table: 'books',
  event: PostgresChangeEvent.insert,
  newRecord: {'id': 1, 'author_id': 2, 'title': 'Dune', 'in_print': true},
);
final change = await changes.first;
expect(change, isA<TypedPostgresInsert<BooksRow, BooksRow>>());
```

A `stream()` test stubs the initial rows with `stubTable` and then emits the
changes it wants applied. `emitBroadcast` delivers a broadcast message,
`emitPresenceState` and `emitPresenceDiff` deliver presence,
`closeConnection` drops the socket, and `sent` and `joinedTopics` record what
the client sent.

## Flutter widget tests

`initializeTestSupabase` from `package:supabase_flutter/testing.dart`
initializes `Supabase.instance` with the same test defaults, so widgets that
read `Supabase.instance.client` work unchanged:

```dart test-file
import 'package:flutter/widgets.dart';
import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';
import 'package:my_app/supabase_schema.g.dart';
import 'package:supabase_flutter/supabase_flutter.dart';
import 'package:supabase_flutter/testing.dart';
import 'package:supabase_test/supabase_test.dart';

class BookTitles extends StatefulWidget {
  const BookTitles({super.key});

  @override
  State<BookTitles> createState() => _BookTitlesState();
}

class _BookTitlesState extends State<BookTitles> {
  final _books = Supabase.instance.client
      .table(Books.table)
      .selectOnly([Books.id, Books.title]);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return FutureBuilder(
      future: _books,
      builder: (context, snapshot) => Column(
        children: [
          for (final book in snapshot.data ?? const [])
            Text(book.read(Books.title), textDirection: TextDirection.ltr),
        ],
      ),
    );
  }
}

void main() {
  testWidgets('shows the book titles', (tester) async {
    final httpClient = MockSupabaseHttpClient()
      ..stubTable(
        'books',
        rows: [
          {'id': 1, 'title': 'Dune'},
        ],
      );
    final supabase = await initializeTestSupabase(httpClient: httpClient);
    addTearDown(supabase.dispose);

    await tester.pumpWidget(const BookTitles());
    await tester.pumpAndSettle();

    expect(find.text('Dune'), findsOneWidget);
  });
}
```

## Integration tests

Mocks are for fast unit tests. For end-to-end coverage, run the tests
against a local stack from `supabase start` and point a regular client at the
URL and keys it prints.
